<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Le Puy-en-Velay</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anicium, Le Puy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Le Puy-en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velay">Velay</a> is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-loire">Haute-Loire</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, built among volcanic pinnacles. As Anicium it replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-paulien">Ruessium</a> as the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of the Velay in late antiquity. Its cathedral with a venerated Black Virgin made it a major pilgrimage centre, and it is the starting point of the Via Podiensis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>. It is also known for lace-making.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay.jpg" alt="Le Puy-en-Velay" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grain de Sel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cath%C3%A9drale_Le_Puy-en-Velay.JPEG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
